w York gets stock prices milliseconds before someone in rural Iowa. A hedge fund pays for faster data feeds. A company insider knows the quarterly earnings a week before they're released. The line between "fair" and "unfair" is already blurry. What I'm suggesting is that we draw the line not at banning insider trading entirely—which doesn't work—but at requiring transparency, so that the information advantage is minimized and made visible. That's not a perfect solution, but I believe it's a more honest and effective one than pretending we can stop insiders from trading at all.
